  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1043 PM MDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nThe slow moving area of low pressure that has kept the last several \ndays cool and wet remains in place around the Four Corners, pumping \nmore tropical moisture into the region. Rain showers, and a few \nthunderstorms, will continue to move from south to north through \neastern Utah and western Colorado through the night tonight. At the \nhigher elevations, light snowfall will be possible above 9-10k feet. \nLittle in the way of accumulations are expected, but it could lead \nto slippery commutes over the southern mountain passes Sunday \nmorning. While rain rates remain on the lower side, these showers \nwill contribute more runoff into the already waterlogged rivers and \nstreams. While a number of rivers have already crested and are on \ntheir way down, a few rivers, particularly the San Juan River \nthrough the Four Corners and Southeast Utah, continue to rise. A \nFlood Watch, and a number of Flood Warnings, remain in place through \nSunday afternoon to capture the expected crests in the next 6-12 \nhours. \n\nDrier air tries to push in from the southwest during the day \ntomorrow as the low finally starts to lift northeast. This will \nbring a break from showers before the cold frontal passage \nreinvigorates showers and storms during the afternoon. These storms \nwill be capable of producing brief heavy rain, frequent lightning, \nsmall hail, and gusty winds. Colder air in the wake of the front and \nlingering moisture will allow for light mountain rain and snow \nshowers to linger Sunday night into Monday. Again, little snow \naccumulation is expected, but passes could once again be slippery \nMonday morning, especially along the Central and Northern Divide. \n\nThis low finally moves east of the Divide by Monday morning, with \nmuch drier conditions expected through the day Monday. A quick \nmoving clipper will just brush our northern border Monday night into \nTuesday, with the potential for some light rain and snow showers \nover the higher terrain of the eastern Uintas and Northern Colorado \nDivide. Behind this clipper, zonal flow takes control, bringing \nmostly quiet conditions and gradually warming temperatures through \nthe back half of the work week. Highs start out 5-10 degrees below \nnormal Sunday through Tuesday, climbing to near-normal by the \nWednesday-Thursday time period. By Friday, flow aloft shifts to a \nmoister southwesterly direction in response to a low deepening off the \nWest Coast, bringing increased cloud cover and a slight cooldown. \nModel agreement is poor beyond this point, but there are indications \nthat we could see a return to cooler and unsettled conditions by \nnext weekend.",
